Z||000000||fiero56||11-22-2000||10:11 AM||||I am ordering new upper ball joints for my ('88)Formula today, and before I stick 'em in, I want to know if I will really need to have an alignment done. Normally I wouldn't hesitate to have it aligned, but I installed poly up front and had it all aligned just a couple of months ago and I don't feel like shelling out the cash for another. Seems to me the camber might be off a tad, but I'm sure it's off right now with those worn out ball joints.<P>Any thought about the alignment?||||reg||5
Z||000001||bHooper||11-22-2000||10:53 AM||||Yes, you need to have it aligned.  Thats why I decided to do my whole car at once... shocks, struts, poly bushings, ball joints, tie rod ends, steering rack, rear rotors, calipers and eibachs all the way around!<P>The upper ball joint has a lot to do with the camber adjustment.  So unless you are a genious (and you might be, Wil E), or damned lucky (possibly) you will need to have the car aligned.<P>Good.<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||11-22-2000||11:56 AM||||I was afraid of that. The main reason I asked, was before I put the poly in, I replaced both lower ball joints, and drove the car for about 5,000 miles without alignment with no problem. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-22-2000||02:01 PM||||Even small alignment errors will affect the car handeling and tire wear. just because you can't feel an issue with the steering wheel doesn't mean it ain't there. You might get lucky and get it inside spec's but you can't be sure without putting in on the machine.<P>||||reg||1
